#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097508} :activity/id #uuid "8fcbc060-700b-5d19-b54b-f889d83b8904" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303 :topic/id #uuid "eae67b9f-eeff-516d-8910-5ef24609f9ed" :topic/math-academy-id 1038} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097922 :tutorial/content "How can we calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\sin x \\cos x \\, \\textrm{d}x?$  \n\nNotice that we have the product of sine and cosine with the same argument $(x).$ In such cases, we can use the double-angle formula for sine to solve the integral.\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for sine, which is\n\n$$\n\\sin {2x}=2\\sin x \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\sin x \\cos x =  \\dfrac{1}{2} \\sin {2x}.\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int \\sin x \\cos x \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin {2x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\int \\sin {2x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cos {2x}\\right )+C \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=-\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cos {2x}+C.\t\n\t}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can use the double-angle formula for sine to solve any integral that's of the form \n\n$$\n\\int \\sin\\left(ax+b\\right)\\cos(ax+b)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n Let's take a look at another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c1eb8ee2-db04-591c-b7f9-cd699c4e3573" :tutorial/math-academy-id 878 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "65464d10-225f-54ed-8f6c-218e4b4d2657" :step/math-academy-id 637993 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096665 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096471 :question/id #uuid "f1021ccd-e37f-5880-baf2-0f0eacd7dd95" :question/math-academy-id "e-481"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4c5541e-8195-53de-b604-e622c3b6a2e7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436670}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"} :step/id #uuid "31f4b71b-4658-5674-ba5b-63ad1748c1a7" :step/math-academy-id 637994 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097510}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097923 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the three double-angle formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the double-angle formulas for cosine to integrate $\\sin^2{x}$ and $\\cos^2{x}.$ Let's take a look at some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "87755d89-cab8-5737-9e44-ae816aefe23f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 880 :tutorial/title "Using the Double-Angle Formulas for Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "de91749b-079a-5aad-bc6d-d965d5964042" :step/math-academy-id 637997 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097513}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097511 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096666 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096472 :question/id #uuid "99b7b82d-431f-5992-9b1e-a80a6b3850c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-482"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b466bd78-724b-51b2-8fee-7991bf7b60d2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250304} {